 ## Overview
-Value compression reduces the storage and bandwidth requirements of volumetric data by transforming scalar fields into a coefficient domain (typically frequency or multi-resolution), quantizing these coefficients, and applying entropy coding [S1][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high memory footprint in large-scale volume rendering by exploiting statistical redundancy and spatial coherence that are not apparent in the raw spatial domain [S6, unofficial]. Unlike block-based methods (e.g., [Texture Format Compression](5623-texture-format-compression.md)) that operate on fixed spatial neighborhoods, value compression methods often provide scalable quality and support multi-resolution rendering through hierarchical coefficient structures [S1, S7].
+Value compression reduces the storage and bandwidth requirements of volumetric data by transforming the scalar field into a set of coefficients, quantizing them, and applying entropy coding [S1][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high memory footprint in large-scale volumetric datasets by exploiting spatial redundancy in the frequency or multi-resolution domain [S1][S6, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings, such as [Structural and Geometric Compression](4959-structural-and-geometric-compression.md) (which targets explicit geometry) and [Predictive and Differential Coding](4393-predictive-and-differential-coding.md) (which operates locally in space/time), by relying on global or block-wise linear transforms (e.g., wavelets, DCT) to decorrelate data before compression [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family share the mechanism of decomposing the input volume $V$ into a set of coefficients $C$ using a linear transform, followed by encoding and optional decoding for rendering.
 
-1.  **Transform:** The volume is decomposed using a separable 1D filter bank applied along each axis. For a 3D volume, this results in subbands representing different frequency components. In wavelet transforms, these are typically approximation (low-pass) and detail (high-pass) coefficients. In Laplacian pyramids, they are detail residuals between levels of a Gaussian pyramid [see [Wavelet and Pyramid Transforms](3856-wavelet-and-pyramid-transforms.md)].
-2.  **Hierarchy:** The decomposition is recursive. The approximation subband from level $j$ is further decomposed at level $j+1$, creating a tree-like structure of coefficients. This allows for multi-resolution access, where coarse data is available at the root and fine details are deeper in the tree [see [Value compression](6-value-compression.md)].
-3.  **Quantization and Entropy Encoding:** Coefficients are quantized (lossy) or kept precise (lossless). Entropy coding (e.g., Huffman, Arithmetic, Run-Length) is applied to the quantized or raw coefficients to produce the compressed bitstream [see [Value compression](6-value-compression.md)].
-4.  **Reconstruction:** To render, the necessary coefficients are decoded and the inverse transform is applied. This can be done globally (full reconstruction) or locally (block-wise or point-wise) to support adaptive resolution rendering [see [Value compression](6-value-compression.md)].
+1. **Transform:** The volume is decomposed using a separable 1D filter bank applied along each axis. For a 3D volume, this results in subbands representing different frequency components. In wavelet transforms, these are typically approximation (low-pass) and detail (high-pass) coefficients. In Laplacian pyramids, they are detail residuals between levels of a Gaussian pyramid [see [Wavelet and Pyramid Transforms](3856-wavelet-and-pyramid-transforms.md)].
+2. **Hierarchy:** The decomposition is recursive. The approximation subband from level $j$ is further decomposed at level $j+1$, creating a tree-like structure of coefficients. This allows for multi-resolution access, where coarse data is available at the root and fine details are deeper in the tree [see [Value compression](6-value-compression.md)].
