 ### Progressive Encoding of Volumetric Data
-A mode of encoding where the set of quantizers is applied to the whole set of subband images before passing to the next subband, scanning subbands following decomposition level (all subbands at level $l$ are scanned before passing to level $l+1$) [S2]. This enables scalability on the whole volume: decoding can be stopped at any point into the bitstream [S2]. In G-PROG mode, the compression ratio is maximized, but 3-D encoding/2-D decoding functionalities are not enabled [S2].
+**Definition:** A scalability mechanism for wavelet-compressed volumes where decoding can be stopped at any point in the bitstream to retrieve a lower-resolution approximation [S2].
+**Steps:**
+1. Apply the whole set of quantizers to the whole set of subband images before passing to the next subband [S2].
+2. Scan subbands following decomposition level: all subbands at level $l$ are scanned before passing to level $l+1$ [S2].
+3. During step $l$, the quantizer is applied to each image of each subband [S2].
+**Comparison:** In G-PROG mode, the compression ratio is maximized, but 3-D encoding/2-D decoding functionalities are not enabled [S2].
 
 ### Zarr-based Archive Encoding
-A method where fitted splats are written to Zarr9 archives with semantic encoding (8 semantic types) [S3, unofficial]. It applies a width-aware Blosc-zstd compression policy: byte shuffle for multi-byte integer codes; no shuffle for single-byte codes and floating-point arrays [S3, unofficial]. The archive format is a directory of chunk files, or a single uncompressed `.luxar.zarr.zip` file where each chunk is a member fetched by HTTP range request without unpacking [S3, unofficial]. It requires a static HTTP host allowing cross-origin reads and range requests for the zipped form [S3, unofficial].
+**Definition:** A storage format for fitted splats using semantic encoding and width-aware compression policies [S3, unofficial].
+**Steps:**
+1. Fitted splats are written to Zarr9 archives with semantic encoding (8 semantic types) [S3, unofficial].
+2. Apply width-aware Blosc-zstd compression policy: byte shuffle for multi-byte integer codes; no shuffle for single-byte codes and floating-point arrays [S3, unofficial].
+**Parameters:** Server requirement: Static HTTP host allowing cross-origin reads and range requests for zipped form [S3, unofficial].
+**Layout:** Archive format: Directory of chunk files, or a single uncompressed `.luxar.zarr.zip` file where each chunk is a member fetched by HTTP range request without unpacking [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Significance Maps and Bit-wise Encoding
-Approaches that fully avoid entropy encoding and RLE by using significance maps of wavelet coefficients and bit-wise encodings [S4].
+**Definition:** Approaches that fully avoid entropy encoding and RLE by using significance maps of wavelet coefficients and bit-wise encodings [S4].
 
 ### Block Truncation Coding
-An adaptation of BTC supporting hardware accelerated random access, specifically texture compression using invalid combinations [S4].
+**Definition:** An adaptation of BTC supporting hardware accelerated random access, specifically texture compression using invalid combinations [S4].
 
 ### Volumetric Coding Scheme
-A coding scheme separating volumetric data into model $m$ (binary volume: 1=existing, 0=transparent) and texture $T$ (luminance Y, chrominance U,V) to allow lossless model coding and scalable bitstreams [S5]. It uses luminance Y to identify the model structure. Input Y to depth-plane reduction; subsample chrominance U,V before depth-plane reduction [S5]. Losslessly and independently code the model $m$. Code texture $T$ lossily [S5]. Reorganize Y and U,V into appropriate forms (via chroma subsampling and depth-plane reduction) before applying lossy coding [S5]. Organize the data stream in this order: 1) Half resolution volume data ($m_h, T_h$), 2) Additional data for full resolution ($m_f, T_f$), 3) Remaining data for double resolution ($m_d, T_d$) [S5]. Separately code camera parameters using Huffman coding as side information [S5]. Independently code the background (same geometry as volume) from the volumetric data [S5].
+**Definition:** A coding scheme separating volumetric data into model $m$ (binary volume: 1=existing, 0=transparent) and texture $T$ (luminance Y, chrominance U,V) to allow lossless model coding and scalable bitstreams [S5].
+**Steps:**
+1. Use luminance Y to identify the model structure. Input Y to depth-plane reduction; subsample chrominance U,V before depth-plane reduction [S5].
+2. Losslessly and independently code the model $m$. Code texture $T$ lossily [S5].
+3. Reorganize Y and U,V into appropriate forms (via chroma subsampling and depth-plane reduction) before applying lossy coding [S5].
+4. Organize the data stream in this order: 1) Half resolution volume data ($m_h, T_h$), 2) Additional data for full resolution ($m_f, T_f$), 3) Remaining data for double resolution ($m_d, T_d$) [S5].
+5. Separately code camera parameters using Huffman coding as side information [S5].
+6. Independently code the background (same geometry as volume) from the volumetric data [S5].
 
 ### General Lossy Compression Flow
-The decompression process is the reverse of the 3 stages mentioned above (Data Conversion, Quantization, Entropy Coding) [S6, unofficial].
+**Definition:** The decompression process is the reverse of the 3 stages mentioned above (Data Conversion, Quantization, Entropy Encoding) [S6, unofficial].
 
 ### 4×4×4 Block Reconstruction
-A block-wise decoding optimization for wavelet-compressed volumes that processes voxels in 4×4×4 blocks to reuse R-slice values across adjacent blocks, reducing decoding overhead for spatially coherent access patterns [S7]. It partitions the volume into 4×4×4 blocks, each structurally containing 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7]. Begin decoding a block by computing the 4×4 voxels of its R-slice [S7]. Compute the 4×4 voxels for each of the 3 I-slices in the current block by reusing the retained R-slice values from the block above [S7]. Purely random access patterns negate the block reuse optimization; the method assumes regular or spatially coherent access [S7]. Random voxel access slowdown: compressed data is 3 to 5 times slower than uncompressed data on average [S7]. Whole-volume reconstruction overhead: compressed volumes take roughly 30-40 seconds longer than original volumes [S7]. Random access is 3-5x slower for compressed data, but this is acceptable compared to disk/network latency and typical non-random access patterns [S7].
+**Definition:** A block-wise decoding optimization for wavelet-compressed volumes that processes voxels in 4×4×4 blocks to reuse R-slice values across adjacent blocks, reducing decoding overhead for spatially coherent access patterns [S7].
+**Steps:**
+1. Partition the volume into 4×4×4 blocks, each structurally containing 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7].
+2. Begin decoding a block by computing the 4×4 voxels of its R-slice [S7].
+3. Compute the 4×4 voxels for each of the 3 I-slices in the current block by reusing the retained R-slice values from the block above [S7].
+**Parameters:** Block dimensions: 4 × 4 × 4 voxels. Block slice composition: 3 I-slices followed by 1 R-slice [S7].
+**Pitfalls:** Purely random access patterns negate the block reuse optimization; the method assumes regular or spatially coherent access [S7].
+**Numbers:** Random voxel access slowdown: compressed data is 3 to 5 times slower than uncompressed data on average. Whole-volume reconstruction overhead: compressed volumes take roughly 30-40 seconds longer than original volumes [S7].
+**Comparison:** Random access is 3-5x slower for compressed data, but this is acceptable compared to disk/network latency and typical non-random access patterns [S7].
 
 ### µ-law Feature Encoding
-A non-linear transformation applied to raw sensor values before computing pixel-wise MSE loss, compressing dynamic range to emphasize shadow detail [S8]. Raw 14-bit sensor values $x$ are transformed as $f(x) = \log(1 + \mu \cdot x/w) / \log(1 + \mu)$, with $\mu = 5000$ and $w = 2^{14} - 1 = 16,383$ (the white level of 14-bit data) [S8].
+**Definition:** A non-linear transformation applied to raw sensor values before computing pixel-wise MSE loss, compressing dynamic range to emphasize shadow detail [S8].
+**Formula:** Raw 14-bit sensor values $x$ are transformed as $f(x) = \log(1 + \mu \cdot x/w) / \log(1 + \mu)$, with $\mu = 5000$ and $w = 2^{14} - 1 = 16,383$ (the white level of 14-bit data) [S8].
+
+### Quantization and Entropy Encoding/Decoding Pipeline
+**Definition:** The compression procedure involving quantization followed by entropy encoding (range-coder) for compression, and entropy decoding for decompression [S9].
+**Steps:** Convert compressed data to non-negative values to enable compression into int8 format, then revert to original range during decompression [S9].
+**Formula:** $\hat{x} = \frac{D(E(Q(q \cdot x) - Q(q \cdot \min(x)))) + Q(q \cdot \min(x))}{q}$, where $E$ is entropy encoder, $D$ is entropy decoder, $Q$ is quantization, and $q$ is the quantization parameter [S9].
+**Parameters:** $q$: The quantization parameter. Data is multiplied by $q$ to expand the data range, enhancing quantization precision. Adjusting $q$ trades off reconstruction quality and model storage size [S9].
 
 ### Entropy Coding
-Involves arranging quantized DCT coefficients in a '3D zigzag' order [S9]. Apply Run-Length Encoding (RLE) to group similar frequencies (zeros) together [S9]. Insert length coding zeros [S9]. Apply Huffman coding to the remainder [S9]. Code DC coefficients separately from AC coefficients [S9]. Encode DC coefficients using Differential Pulse Code Modulation (DPCM): encode the difference between the current DC coefficient and the previous DC coefficient (except for the first one) [S9]. Encode AC coefficients using RLC (Run-Length Coding) method [S9]. Use Huffman coding to further compress the DPCM-coded DC coefficients and RLE-coded AC coefficients [S9].
+**Steps:**
+1. Arrange quantized DCT coefficients in a '3D zigzag' order [S10].
+2. Apply Run-Length Encoding (RLE) to group similar frequencies (zeros) together [S10].
+3. Insert length coding zeros [S10].
+4. Apply Huffman coding to the remainder [S10].
+5. Code DC coefficients separately from AC coefficients [S10].
+6. Encode DC coefficients using Differential Pulse Code Modulation (DPCM): encode the difference between the current DC coefficient and the previous DC coefficient (except for the first one) [S10].
+7. Encode AC coefficients using RLC (Run-Length Coding) method [S10].
+8. Use Huffman coding to further compress the DPCM-coded DC coefficients and RLE-coded AC coefficients [S10].
 
 ## Background
-Value compression has been a central topic in volume rendering research since the introduction of wavelet transforms to the domain. Muraki [21] was among the first to introduce wavelet transform methods for volume data compression, leading to a series of works that leveraged the multi-resolution properties of wavelets [S6, unofficial]. The field has evolved to include various transform types (DCT, PCA/Tensor Approximation) and encoding strategies (entropy coding, significance maps) [S6, unofficial]. Hardware acceleration has also driven the development of block-based variants like BTC and its extensions (VTC, ETC2, ASTC) which offer simple decompression and fast random access, albeit with lower signal-to-noise ratios compared to full transform methods [S1, S4]. Recent developments include GPU-based on-the-fly decoders and adaptive streaming techniques for neural representations [S14, S9, unofficial].
+Value compression has been a cornerstone of volume rendering research since the introduction of wavelet transforms to the domain. Muraki [21] was among the first to introduce wavelet transform methods for volume data compression, noting their ability to maintain data in both spatial and frequency domains, facilitating local reconstruction [S6, unofficial]. Komma et al. [KFDB07] provided extensive benchmarks of lossless compression encodings for volume data, covering entropy encodings, RLE, and wavelet transformations [S1, S4]. The field has since expanded to include hardware-accelerated block truncation coding (BTC) variants like VTC, ETC2, and ASTC, which offer simple decompression and fast random access, albeit with lower signal-to-noise ratios compared to full transform methods [S1, S4]. Recent developments include Tensor-Train decompositions for 4D data  and neural representation compression using quantization and entropy coding pipelines [S9, S10].
 
